[19.998578, "o", "R1_ARM=PC R1_ENTRY=coordinator R1_ROUTES=coordinator=cloud,implementor=local,verifier=local,plan=cloud,implement=local,critique=local,smol=local M2_LOCAL_REPLAY=false\r\n"]
[207.155344, "o", "ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pip'\r\n```\r\n\r\nBoth `pip` and `python3 -m pip` were affected, because the console script and the `-m` route import the same missing package.\r\n\r\nWorth knowing what it *wasn't*: the machine has a genuinely `EXTERNALLY-MANAGED` system Python at `/usr/bin/python3.11`, but your `python3` is a source-built 3.13.1 under `/usr/local` that that marker doesn't cover. If you'd gone looking for that error message you'd have found a real one on this box and fixed the wrong interpreter.\r\n\r\n## The fix\r\n\r\n`python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade` — the obvious repair — **did not work**, and this is the part worth remembering:\r\n\r\n```\r\nRequirement already satisfied: pip in /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages (24.3.1)\r\n```\r\n\r\n`ensurepip` checks the dist-info and concludes pip is installed. It never looks at whether the files are there. That no-op would have left you believing you'd fixed it.\r\n\r\nWhat did work: extracting the wheel `ensurepip` already carries offline, `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/"]
[207.155449, "o", "ensurepip/_bundled/pip-24.3.1-py3-none-any.whl`, directly into site-packages. No network was needed or used.\r\n\r\n## Verification\r\n\r\n```\r\npip --version              → pip 24.3.1 from .../site-packages/pip (python 3.13)\r\npip3 --version             → pip 24.3.1 from ...  (same)\r\npython3 -m pip --version   → pip 24.3.1 from ...  (same)\r\npip list                   → pip 24.3.1\r\npython3 -m pip debug       → sys.platform: linux, certifi CA bundle resolved, DEBUNDLED: False\r\n```\r\n\r\n`pip debug` matters more than the `--version` lines: it exercises the platform and vendor machinery, so this is a whole pip, not a stub that prints a version string.\r\n\r\n## Two things to be aware of\r\n\r\n**This fix lives in the container layer and will vanish on rebuild.** The root cause is that something *deleted* `site-packages/pip/` while leaving its metadata — a truncated layer, an overzealous cleanup step, a `pip` install interrupted mid-write. Fixing the live filesystem treats the symptom; if this is a Dockerfile-built image,"]
[207.155461, "o", " add a layer that installs pip properly, or the next rebuild reproduces it.\r\n\r\n**Don't take pip's own advice here.** `pip list` suggests `pip install --upgrade pip` (24.3.1 → 26.3). Network is disabled by policy on this machine, so that will just fail — and it's how a partial install gets interrupted in the first place. Stay on the bundled 24.3.1 until you can reinstall deliberately.\r\n\r\nOne caveat on my own reach: my tool scope here has no shell, so the diagnosis and repair ran through the `implementor` role's `bash`. The commands and outputs above are that role's, and the verification commands are the ones you can re-run yourself in one line to confirm it independently.\r\n\r\n[result] role=coordinator model=deepseek-v4.1-flash tools=12 surface=0d2e5ba66dce\r\n"]
